<p>
    <strong>HTML</strong> stands for HyperText Markup Language. You write words and put them
    in something called a <strong>tag</strong> that tell the browser what each part of the website is like a heading, paragraph, etc.
</p>

<h2>Parts of a tag</h2>
<pre>&lt;p class="intro"&gt;Hello&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<ul>
    <li><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> is an <strong>opening tag</strong>.It tells the browser where content begins. The p stands for paragraph.</li>
    <li><code>class="intro"</code> is an <strong>attribute</strong>. What it does is add more information to the current tag and adjusts its behaviour.</li>
    <li><code>Hello, world!</code> is the <strong>words or images</strong> that you see on the page.</li>
    <li><code>&lt;/p&gt;</code> is the <strong>closing tag</strong>.Which tells the browser where to end the content. The slash means to end here.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Most used tags</h2>
<table>
    <tr><th>Tag</th><th>What it makes</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code></td><td>Headings, from largest to smallest</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;p&gt;</code></td><td>paragraphs</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;a href="..."&gt;</code></td><td>links to other pages or websites</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;img src="..." alt="..."&gt;</code></td><td>pictures or illustrations/images</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code></td><td>makes bullet points</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code></td><td>creates numbered lists</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;strong&gt;</code></td><td>Bolded text</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;div&gt;</code></td><td>A box that contains content and groups it.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Tags inside of tags</h2>
<p>
    Tags can also be inside of other tags. Which is called <strong>nesting</strong>.Also you always close the
    innermost tag before the outermost tag.
</p>
<pre>
&lt;!--correct--&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is called &lt;n&gt;nesting&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;!--Wrong--&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is called &lt;&gt;nesting&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;

</pre>
<p>Lines that begin with <code>&lt;!--</code> are called <strong>comments</strong>.
 Which the browser ignores and does not show when it showsthe page.</p>

<h2>A page</h2>
<pre>
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;3rd Page&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;This a web page.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</pre>
<ul>
    <li>The <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> code has the information about the page like the title. It acts as a container for data on the page.</li>
    <li>The <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> is everything you see on the page.</li>
</ul>

<p class="note">
    <strong>Do you know why &amp;lt; is in the code?</strong> Well the browser thinks <code>&lt;</code> starts
    a tag instead of plain text. To show it as plain text, HTML has to use <code>&amp;lt;</code> for &lt; and
    <code>&amp;gt;</code> for &gt; so that you don't end up making an error.
